# Rust name resolution


## What names to import

- Basically, import the module-level items; e.g. types, traits, constants
- If you think the identifier you are going to import is too generic, import its module name instead

```rust
// GOOD - importing the names under the module
use ::std::ops::{Add, Range};

// BAD - importing the names under enum
use ::std::borrow::Cow::Borrowed;

// GOOD - using `take` with its module name to clarify which `take` we are using
use ::std::mem;
mem::take(...);

// BAD - `take` is too generic name to import
use ::std::mem::take;
```

## Path roots in `use`

- Current crate: `crate::...` or `self::...`
- Anything else (std/core/alloc, deps, other workspace crates): must start with `::`

```rust
// GOOD
use crate::fields::wire::ProtoType;
use ::core::mem::ManuallyDrop;
use ::bytes::{Buf, BufMut};
use ::puroro::DecodeError;
use ::std::mem;

// BAD — missing `::` for an external crate
use bytes::Buf;
use std::io::Read;
```

## Name `Result`

The name `Result` is a little special because typically a library defines its own `Result` type.
So here's the rule:

1. If the currently working crate is defining its own `Result` type, then *no other `Result` name should be imported without aliasing*. Otherwise you can import a single `Result` type per file.
2. You can, though, import any `Result` type freely if you alias it.
3. When you want to alias other crate's name `Result`, prefix it by a short name of the module or crate. e.g. `::std::result::Result` can be aliased to `StdResult`.

## When `clippy::absolute_paths` fires

1. Decide what name you want to use instead. It might be a bare Identifier (`Deref`), or a name with its module name (`ops::Deref`).
2. Add a `use` with the correct root (`crate::` / `self::` / `::...`) which is needed for your name.
3. Replace the inline path with the new name.
4. Do not silence the lint unless:
  1. the code is machine-generated and intentionally excluded from workspace lints.
  2. you are confident that the identifier is confusing even if the module name is appended.

```rust
// BAD — inline absolute path
fn open() -> ::std::io::Result<::std::fs::File> {
    ::std::fs::File::open("data.bin")
}

// GOOD
use ::std::fs::File;
use ::std::io::Result;

fn open() -> Result<File> {
    File::open("data.bin")
}
```

## Exceptions

- Macro-generated code may keep absolute paths; Clippy already skips many macro expansions.
- Doc examples should still follow the same `use` / `::` style when they are project-owned.
